Robert Shaw wrote:
> Derek Ray wrote:
>> Robert Shaw wrote:
>>> Rubbing non rusty armour could polish it, giving a 5% chance of
>>> reflection, once only.

^^ note original

>> A more proper balance would be ...(snipped)
> 
> I didn't mean intrinsic. I meant something more like greased,
> which can wear off. Polish up the armour, and it'll reflect
> one spell, maybe two, before losing its shine. Alternately,
> it has a chance of getting scuffed everytime you're hit,
> averaging perhaps a dozen turns.

It would probably be best to make these things clearer in the original
suggestion.  "5% chance, once only", to me, means that your armor always
becomes polished after you #rub it, and polished armor cannot be
effectively #rubbed -- thus making the percentile chance meaningful.

If I can #rub as much as I like and it's a one-shot deal, then this
starts to fall over the edge into simultaneously being "too easy" and
"worthless in the crunch".  When I'm tackling Ludios, for example (a
time when reflection is really needed), I might as well not bother since
spending turns to re-#rub in melee combat is usually going to be a bad
way to go -- and soldiers will, of course, gleefully zap every time
until their wands are empty.  But on the other end of the scale,
wandering around ye Dungeon Proper, I will nearly always have time to
stop and make sure my #rub protection is active after getting
incidentally zapped with something.  Even if it's only a 5% chance per
rub, it'll be well worth my time to waste 20 turns turning reflection
"back on", just so the only warning I get about that gnome with a wand
of death isn't DYWYPI?

The scuffed-when-hit thing is probably too strong, especially for
classes that prefer ranged attacks in the first place and thus largely
don't even GET hit -- and anyone can throw daggers.

